**Version:** backpass 0.1.30; the same code is still in 0.1.32.
**Setup.** A shared skill library with per-harness symlinks (the layout the `loadSkills` comment calls
"the common way to share skills across harnesses"): 154 skills live in `~/.agents/skills`, and
`~/.claude/skills/<name>` and `~/.codex/skills/<name>` are symlinks to them. User-scope defaults load
all three roots (`USER_SKILLS_DIRS`).

**Problem.** `resolveProjectSkillDirs` dedupes roots by realpath, but the roots are distinct real
directories, so each shared skill is loaded three times. `renderSkillIndexForAnalysis` then prints
every copy. In our case the "Project skills (load-on-trigger)" section was 567 lines and ~187k chars
for 199 unique skills - 88% of each analysis prompt, paid on every transcript.

**Suggested fix.** Keep `loadedCopies` / description-budget accounting as is (each copy is billed on
purpose), but dedupe only the index shown to the analysis model, e.g. by realpath of the skill file
or by name + description, first path wins:

```js
export function renderSkillIndexForAnalysis(skills) {
  if (!skills.length) return "(this repo has no skills)";
  const seen = new Set();
  const unique = skills.filter((s) => {
    const key = `${s.name}\^@${s.description || ""}`;
    if (seen.has(key)) return false;
    seen.add(key);
    return true;
  });
  return unique.map((s) => `- ${s.name} (${s.path}) :: ${s.description || "(no description)"}`).join("\n");
}
```

**Measured effect** (2 transcripts, `backpass analyze --scope user`, claude-sonnet-5-5): skill section
187k -> 77k chars, 199 entries each listed once, first-turn tokens per session -43.5% on an equal basis.

Possibly related to #75 (large skill stores inflating prompts), but a different cause: here it is per-skill symlinks across harness roots, not the copied tree.

## Intent

Fix issue #203: with the claude analysis agent at --jobs above 1, the first wave of parallel calls each starts with a short-lived `acpx claude sessions new` helper; when Claude's OAuth token is due a helper can abandon .oauth_refresh.lock and every session fails in about 15 seconds. As the maintainer and reporter suggest, run the first Claude analysis call alone, start to finish, before the fan-out so a due refresh finishes inside one long-lived process. Not a retry loop and not a quieter degraded path. Closes `#203`.

## What Changed

- `analyzeOne` now tags a thrown error with `modelCallAttempted` so callers can tell a failure that reached the model call apart from one that never got that far (e.g. a transcript that failed to read or distill).
- `analyzeTranscripts` extracts the per-transcript work into `analyzePending`, which now returns an outcome (`ok`, `skipped`, `failed`, or `failed-attempted`). When the agent is `claude` and `--jobs` is above 1, it runs transcripts one at a time, synchronously, until one outcome shows a model call was made (`ok` or `failed-attempted`), then runs the remaining transcripts through the normal `pool` fan-out. This lets a due OAuth refresh finish inside a single long-lived process instead of each parallel `acpx claude sessions new` helper abandoning the refresh lock.
- Adds `test/analyze-claude-warmup.test.js` and `test/analyze-warmup-write-fault.test.js` covering warm-up detection of model-call attempts (success and failure) and the write-fault case where `writeEvidence` throws after a model call succeeds.

## Risk Assessment

✅ Low: This fix round correctly addresses the round-1 finding (outcome overwrite when writeEvidence throws after a successful model call, by moving the `outcome = "ok"` assignment and counters after the writeEvidence call so a later throw there is now correctly classified as `failed-attempted`, keeping `called` true). The round-2 finding about pre-call I/O errors inside analyzeOne's try block being marked `modelCallAttempted` was explicitly declined by the user and remains unchanged, which is correct per instructions. The warm-up loop's gating (`pick.agent === "claude" && config.jobs > 1`), serial processing until an actual model call or call-attempt occurs, and fallthrough to the normal pool afterward all match the stated intent (#203) and the new test exercises real CLI behavior end-to-end with an independent oracle (observed call ordering in a log), not source-text matching.

## Testing

Drove the real CLI (fake-acpx harness) and the real analyzeTranscripts pipeline (in-process, real session files, no mocked internals of the function under test) across four scenarios: the happy-path warm-up serialization, the trivial-session exclusion, non-Claude agents keeping full fan-out, and a new adversarial case where the first transcript's model call succeeds but its evidence write throws (ENOSPC-style). All four passed on HEAD; the fourth was confirmed to fail against the pre-fix outcome computation and pass against the fix, proving it actually exercises the fix. No regressions in the surrounding analyze.js test suite (49/49 passing).

